 "source": "Laozi 老子, 道德經 (Daodejing), chapter 1, in the Wang Bi 王弼 recension (華亭張氏原本, 晉 王弼注), as transcribed at Chinese Wikisource; verified character-for-character against the printed public-domain edition of Paul Carus, Lao-Tze's Tao-Teh-King: Chinese-English (Chicago: Open Court, 1898), p. 53. The two witnesses agree in every character; they differ only in editorial punctuation (Carus points with 。 throughout) and in one variant glyph (Carus prints 衆 for 眾). All punctuation is editorial — the original has none. Chapter division into 81 is traditional. Translations are made fresh from the Chinese. Pronunciation convention: modern Mandarin, given in Hanyu Pinyin — an admitted anachronism, and the living convention for reading these texts, exactly as Ancient Greek is read in a reconstructed classroom standard. Where an Old Chinese rhyme is invisible in Mandarin, a note says so.",
 "about": "The opening chapter of the Daodejing. The received text is Wang Bi's (3rd c. AD); manuscripts found at Mawangdui (1973) and Guodian (1993) are older and differ in places, and the notes flag those differences where they matter. Classical Chinese has no inflection, no obligatory subject, and no fixed parts of speech — the notes show how a line of six characters produces its meaning. Read the characters; the pinyin under each one is a reading aid you can hide with the transcription button.",
