   "l": "Way that can be way-ed — not the abiding way.\nName that can be named — not the abiding name.",
   "i": "A way that can be laid out in words is not the way that abides; a name that can be pronounced is not the name that abides.",
   "n": "Two things to notice, and you will meet both on every page. First, there is no verb 'to be': 非常道 is simply 'not | abiding | way', and English has to supply the 'is'. Second, Classical Chinese does not mark parts of speech — the second 道 in 道可道 is the same character doing duty as a verb, 'to speak of'. Six characters, three 道, and only the middle one is a verb. 常 'abiding' is the received reading; the Mawangdui silk manuscripts (buried 168 BC) read 恆 héng instead, which the Han changed everywhere to avoid the personal name of Emperor Wen.",
   "v": true,
   "p": true
  },
