   "l": "Without-name — the beginning of heaven-and-earth.\nHaving-name — the mother of the ten-thousand things.",
   "i": "The nameless is where heaven and earth begin; the named is the mother of all things.",
   "n": "無名 and 有名 are verb phrases ('lack a name' / 'have a name') standing in for nouns — a thing this language does constantly. 之 is the genitive: 天地之始 is 'heaven-and-earth's beginning'. Watch that character: three lines down it will be an object pronoun instead. A genuine crux here: the text is unpunctuated, and some readers since Wang Anshi (11th c.) divide it 無，名天地之始 — 'Non-being is the name of the beginning of heaven and earth.' Wang Bi's commentary supports the division printed here. The Mawangdui manuscripts read 萬物之始 in the first clause, not 天地之始.",
   "v": true
  },
