   "l": "abidingly having desire, thereby to behold its boundaries.",
   "i": "constantly full of desire, and you behold only its outer edges.",
   "n": "妙 miào and 徼 jiào rhyme — and this is one of the few rhymes in the book that survives into Mandarin, so you can hear it. Most of the others you cannot: the poem was composed for a sound system two thousand years older. 徼 is rare and its sense is argued over; Wang Bi glosses it 歸終, 'where things return and come to an end', so: the outer limit at which a thing finally shows itself.",
   "v": true
